Hi Ray.

> Thank you for an excellent outline of the issues involved in the
> professional troubleshooting of network connectivity. I won't argue
> with the details, mainly because I agree with 99% of it, at least
> for the context in which you developed it (paid troubleshooting).
> 
> But I think they are less applicable to getting free help from
> mailing lists, based on several years of experience here and
> elsewhere; I've already given my reasons.

Fair enough.

> I do take actual exception to one thing you wrote. See below.
>
>>> Specifically, I believe that your statement that the approach you
>>> recommend results in "usually guaranteeing to provide all of the
>>> information needed to debug the problem" is incorrect, as is your
>>> conclusion that "the problem is almost always equally diagnosable
>>> with substituted IP addresses".
>
>> I have corrected your first quote to be the one that I believe I
>> gave (you missed the word "usually" from it), and I stand by the
>> resulting claims as a result of long experience.
>
> Here is the full paragraph I quoted from, as it appears in the copy
> of the message I received from the list:
>
>> "To avoid security problems whilst guaranteeing to provide all of
>> the information needed to debug the problem, simply replace each
>> specific IP address with a specific IP address from one of the
>> "reserved ranges". There can be no security problems with those IP
>> addresses since they are not routable outside of your own network."
>
> You will note that you "corrected" my quoting of you by inserting a
> word (and by doing so, a qualification) that was NOT present in your
> original statement. I don't intentionally misquote people, nor did I
> do so inadvertantly in this case.

I stand corrected, and offer my sincere apologies. I will offer in my
defence that when I replied, I did not have my original to hand to refer
to, and mistakenly thought I had included the qualification and you had
accidentally cut it when quoting.

I also apologise for the misleading impression I gave by omitting that
qualification, and this apology is addressed to everybody on the list.

> You did qualify the *second* statement I quoted with "almost
> always", words that you will note I did include when quoting you.

Many thanks for confirming that.

Best wishes from Riley.

